1|🔴 T1 Python Core|Standard Library & To.how_it_works:
- Tests are functions named `test_*` in files named `test_*.py`; pytest finds and runs them automatically
- Plain `assert` statements work — pytest rewrites them to show rich diffs on failure (no `assertEqual` needed)
- `@pytest.fixture` functions provide reusable setup/teardown — request one by adding it as a parameter to your test
- `@pytest.mark.parametrize` runs the same test logic against many input/expected pairs without copy-pasting

```
import pytest

@pytest.fixture
def sample_df():
    return pd.DataFrame({"x": [1, 2, 3]})

def test_sum(sample_df):
    assert sample_df["x"].sum() == 6

@pytest.mark.parametrize("a,b,expected", [(1, 2, 3), (-1, 1, 0), (0, 0, 0)])
def test_add(a, b, expected):
    assert add(a, b) == expected
```

1|🔴 T1 AI Fundamentals|AI Problem Solvin.how_it_works:
Minimax builds a game tree alternating between MAX nodes (your turn — pick the highest-value child) and MIN nodes (opponent's turn — pick the lowest-value child), then backs up values from terminal/leaf states to the root.

```
def minimax(state, depth, maximizing):
    if terminal(state) or depth == 0:
        return evaluate(state)
    if maximizing:
        return max(minimax(child, depth-1, False) for child in successors(state))
    else:
        return min(minimax(child, depth-1, True) for child in successors(state))
```

The chosen move is the one leading to the child with the best minimax value — i.e. the move that's best *assuming the opponent plays optimally against you*. For games too large to search to the end, an `evaluate()` heuristic estimates the value of non-terminal states at a cutoff depth.

Two values are tracked while traversing the tree: `α` (the best value the maximizing player can guarantee so far) and `β` (the best value the minimizing player can guarantee so far). A branch is pruned the moment `α ≥ β` — it can't possibly affect the final decision.

```
def alphabeta(state, depth, alpha, beta, maximizing):
    if terminal(state) or depth == 0:
        return evaluate(state)
    if maximizing:
        value = -inf
        for child in successors(state):
            value = max(value, alphabeta(child, depth-1, alpha, beta, False))
            alpha = max(alpha, value)
            if alpha >= beta:
                break               # PRUNE — beta cutoff: opponent will never let us reach here
        return value
    else:
        # symmetric: track beta, prune on alpha >= beta (alpha cutoff)
        ...
```

With a good move-ordering heuristic (searching the most promising moves first), Alpha-Beta can reduce the effective branching factor from `b` to roughly `√b` — a dramatic speedup that, in chess engines, translates to searching many additional plies deep in the same time budget.

1|🟡 T2 🔬 Concurrency & Performance|Concu.how_it_works:
PyO3 provides macros and types (`#[pyfunction]`, `#[pymodule]`, `PyResult`, `Python<'_>`) that map Rust functions and structs onto Python-callable equivalents, handling the GIL, reference counting, and type conversions safely under the hood:

```rust
use pyo3::prelude::*;

#[pyfunction]
fn sum_as_string(a: i64, b: i64) -> PyResult<String> {
    Ok((a + b).to_string())
}

#[pymodule]
fn my_rust_module(_py: Python, m: &PyModule) -> PyResult<()> {
    m.add_function(wrap_pyfunction!(sum_as_string, m)?)?;
    Ok(())
}
```

`maturin` then handles the entire build-and-package pipeline — compiling the Rust code, linking against the right Python ABI, and producing a standard `.whl` file installable via `pip`/`uv` exactly like any pure-Python package:

```bash
maturin develop     # build and install into the current virtualenv for local development
maturin build       # produce a distributable wheel
maturin publish     # build and upload to PyPI
```

1|🟡 T2 🔬 New & Deep Research Topics|Pyth.how_it_works:
Every class is an instance of a metaclass — by default `type`. Writing `class Foo(metaclass=Meta)` causes Python to call `Meta(name, bases, namespace)` to construct `Foo` itself, giving `Meta.__new__`/`Meta.__init__` a chance to inspect or rewrite the class before it even exists.

```python
class Meta(type):
    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, ns):
        if "required_method" not in ns:
            raise TypeError(f"{name} must define required_method")
        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, ns)

class Plugin(metaclass=Meta):
    def required_method(self): ...
```

In modern Python, many classic metaclass use cases (registries, validation, attribute injection) are now better served by `__init_subclass__` (a simpler classmethod hook fired on subclassing) or class decorators — both achieve similar ends with far less conceptual overhead.

`ExceptionGroup` wraps a list of exceptions plus a descriptive message; `except*` matches and "splits" a group by exception type, handling matching sub-exceptions while letting non-matching ones propagate in a *new*, smaller group:

```python
try:
    raise ExceptionGroup("multiple failures", [ValueError("bad input"), TypeError("wrong type"), ValueError("also bad")])
except* ValueError as eg:
    print(f"Handled {len(eg.exceptions)} ValueErrors")   # catches both ValueErrors, bundled in their own sub-group
except* TypeError as eg:
    print(f"Handled {len(eg.exceptions)} TypeErrors")    # catches the TypeError separately
```

This is precisely the mechanism `asyncio.TaskGroup` (see [[asyncio.TaskGroup nursery pattern]]) uses to report failures: if multiple tasks in a group raise, `TaskGroup` collects them all into one `ExceptionGroup` instead of silently dropping all but one — ensuring no concurrent failure goes unnoticed.

Wrap any `await`-ing code in `async with asyncio.timeout(seconds):` — if the block hasn't completed when the timer expires, the code inside is cancelled and a `TimeoutError` is raised at the `async with` boundary:

```python
async def fetch_with_timeout(url):
    try:
        async with asyncio.timeout(5.0):
            return await fetch(url)
    except TimeoutError:
        return None   # gracefully handle the slow/hung request
```

Unlike `asyncio.wait_for(coro, timeout)` — which wraps a *single coroutine* and has historically had subtle issues with cleanup and cancellation-scope edge cases — `asyncio.timeout` wraps an arbitrary *block* of code (potentially containing several awaits, or even a `TaskGroup`), composes more predictably with structured concurrency, and even supports dynamically rescheduling the deadline via `.reschedule()`.

Decorate a base function with `@singledispatch`, then register type-specific implementations with `@base_function.register`:

```python
from functools import singledispatch

@singledispatch
def render(value):
    return str(value)            # fallback implementation for unregistered types

@render.register
def _(value: int) -> str:
    return f"Integer: {value}"

@render.register
def _(value: list) -> str:
    return f"List of {len(value)} items"

render(42)        # "Integer: 42"      — dispatches on the argument's type
render([1, 2, 3]) # "List of 3 items"
render(3.14)      # "3.14"             — falls through to the base implementation
```

`singledispatchmethod` applies the identical pattern to instance/class methods, dispatching on the type of the first argument *after* `self`. Critically, new types can be registered from **anywhere** — including other modules, long after the original function was defined — enabling truly extensible, plugin-friendly designs.

`@cache` wraps a function so subsequent calls with identical (hashable) arguments skip computation entirely and return the stored result:

```python
from functools import cache, lru_cache, cached_property

@cache                          # unbounded cache — simplest case
def fib(n):
    return n if n < 2 else fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)   # turns exponential naive recursion into linear time

@lru_cache(maxsize=128)         # bounded cache — evicts least-recently-used entries once full
def expensive_lookup(key): ...

class Report:
    @cached_property
    def summary(self):          # computed once per instance, on first access; stored in __dict__ thereafter
        return expensive_aggregate(self.data)
```

`cached_property` works by storing the computed value directly into the instance's `__dict__` under the property's name — subsequent attribute lookups find it there directly (an ordinary attribute, no recomputation, and crucially no descriptor overhead on the cached path).

1|🟡 T2 🔬 New & Deep Research Topics|Adva.how_it_works:
Define `__init_subclass__` as a classmethod on a base class — Python automatically calls it (passing keyword arguments from the subclass's definition line) every time a new subclass is created:

```python
class Plugin:
    registry = {}

    def __init_subclass__(cls, *, plugin_name=None, **kwargs):
        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)
        if plugin_name:
            Plugin.registry[plugin_name] = cls

class CSVPlugin(Plugin, plugin_name="csv"):
    ...

print(Plugin.registry)   # {'csv': <class 'CSVPlugin'>} — registered automatically, no decorator or metaclass needed
```

This single hook covers the overwhelming majority of cases that used to require a metaclass — auto-registration, enforcing that subclasses implement required methods or attributes, injecting computed class-level data — with dramatically simpler, more readable code.

When a class body executes, for every class attribute that is a descriptor, Python calls `descriptor.__set_name__(owner_class, attribute_name)` — letting the descriptor record its own name (and the class that owns it) for later use in `__get__`/`__set__`:

```python
class Field:
    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):
        self.name = name
        self.private_name = f"_{name}"

    def __get__(self, instance, owner):
        return getattr(instance, self.private_name, None)

    def __set__(self, instance, value):
        setattr(instance, self.private_name, value)

class User:
    username = Field()    # Field.__set_name__ is called automatically: name="username", private_name="_username"
```

This is precisely how libraries like `attrs`, `SQLAlchemy`, and `dataclasses`-adjacent tooling implement descriptor-based fields that "just know" their own attribute name — without the user ever having to write `Field("username")` redundantly.

The essential difference is *enforcement timing and mechanism*:

```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
from typing import Protocol

# Nominal (ABC): explicit inheritance required; enforced at INSTANTIATION time
class Drawable(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def draw(self) -> None: ...

class Circle(Drawable):           # MUST inherit; Python refuses to instantiate without draw()
    def draw(self): print("○")

# Structural (Protocol): no inheritance needed; checked STATICALLY by type checkers
class HasDraw(Protocol):
    def draw(self) -> None: ...

class Square:                      # satisfies HasDraw just by having the right method — no inheritance
    def draw(self): print("□")

def render(item: HasDraw) -> None: item.draw()
render(Square())                   # type-checks fine; Square never mentioned HasDraw
```

`ABC` gives you a runtime guarantee ("you cannot construct an incomplete `Drawable`") at the cost of requiring explicit coupling; `Protocol` gives you static-type-checking flexibility ("anything shaped right works") without that coupling, but enforces nothing at runtime unless marked `@runtime_checkable` (and even then, only checks method *presence*, not signatures).

Annotate a predicate function's return type as `TypeGuard[T]` or `TypeIs[T]` — the type checker then narrows the argument's type to `T` (or rules it out) wherever the function returns `True` (or `False`):

```python
from typing import TypeIs

def is_str_list(val: list[object]) -> TypeIs[list[str]]:
    return all(isinstance(x, str) for x in val)

def process(items: list[object]):
    if is_str_list(items):
        items[0].upper()      # type checker now KNOWS items is list[str] here — no error
```

The key difference PEP 742 (`TypeIs`) introduced over the original `TypeGuard`: `TypeIs` requires the check to be *consistent* with the input type (narrowing in both the `True` and `False` branches symmetrically — closer to how `isinstance` behaves), while `TypeGuard` allows asymmetric, more flexible (but less intuitive) narrowing — `TypeIs` is generally the more predictable, recommended choice for straightforward "is this an X" predicates.
